# Checkpoint 3 — THE HEADLINE. Rotation visibly changes the Option B projection.
#
# At t = 0 the ray runs through the rotation axis. At theta = 0 it cuts, in
# order: 30 mm of polyethylene (bar at 180 deg), 40 mm of aluminium (central
# bar), 20 mm of carbon steel (bar at 0 deg). At theta = 30 deg NO ring bar
# intersects the ray at all — every one of the six sits at least 30 mm off the
# line, further than its own radius — so only the 40 mm aluminium core remains.
#
# Indicative expectation (mu/rho for Al and poly taken at literature grade, NOT
# derived by xcom_reference.py — this is a "does it move" checkpoint, not a
# validation claim):
#
#     theta =  0 deg  ->  unscattered fraction ~ 0.11
#     theta = 30 deg  ->  unscattered fraction ~ 0.45
#
# A factor of four. If these two come out equal, the phantom is not rotating.
#
# WATCH OUT: theta = 0 and theta = 60 deg give the SAME count here, because the
# ray then cuts the same 30 mm of poly and 20 mm of steel in the opposite
# order, and exponential attenuation does not care about order. That is correct
# physics, not a stuck geometry. Sampling 0 / 60 / 120 would look like failure.

/run/verbose      0
/event/verbose    0
/tracking/verbose 0

/cttwin/phantom bars
/run/initialize

/cttwin/scan/translation 0 mm

/cttwin/scan/angle  0 deg
/run/beamOn 100000

/cttwin/scan/angle 30 deg
/run/beamOn 100000

# the order-invariance case described above — expect it to match theta = 0
/cttwin/scan/angle 60 deg
/run/beamOn 100000
